MariaDB Service Troubleshooting (KodeKloud Notes)
Scenario

The application cannot connect to the database because the MariaDB service is down on the database server.

Step 1: SSH into the Database Server
ssh peter@stdb01

Become the root user:

sudo su -
Step 2: Check MariaDB Service Status
systemctl status mariadb

or

systemctl is-active mariadb

Possible outputs:

active
inactive
failed
Step 3: Start the Service
systemctl start mariadb

Verify:

systemctl is-active mariadb

Expected:

active
Step 4: If the Service Fails

Check detailed logs:

systemctl status mariadb --no-pager -l
journalctl -xeu mariadb.service --no-pager

These commands usually tell you the exact reason for the failure.

Error Seen in This Lab
chown: changing ownership of '/var/lib/mysql': Operation not permitted

This indicated a permissions/ownership issue with the MariaDB data directory.

Step 5: Check Directory Ownership
ls -ld /var/lib/mysql

Incorrect:

drwxr-xr-x root mysql

Correct:

drwxr-xr-x mysql mysql
Step 6: Fix Ownership
chown -R mysql:mysql /var/lib/mysql

Verify:

ls -ld /var/lib/mysql

Expected:

drwxr-xr-x mysql mysql
Step 7: Start MariaDB Again
systemctl start mariadb

Verify:

systemctl status mariadb

or

systemctl is-active mariadb

Expected:

active
Step 8: Verify Database Files
ls -l /var/lib/mysql

Typical files:

ibdata1
ib_logfile0
ibtmp1
mysql.sock
performance_schema
mysql/

The presence of mysql.sock generally indicates the server has started successfully.

Useful MariaDB Service Commands
Check status
systemctl status mariadb
Check only active state
systemctl is-active mariadb
Start
systemctl start mariadb
Stop
systemctl stop mariadb
Restart
systemctl restart mariadb
Enable at boot
systemctl enable mariadb
Disable at boot
systemctl disable mariadb
