Common Reasons MariaDB Fails to Start
Issue	How to Check	Fix
Service stopped	systemctl status mariadb	systemctl start mariadb
Wrong ownership	ls -ld /var/lib/mysql	chown -R mysql:mysql /var/lib/mysql
Incorrect permissions	ls -l /var/lib/mysql	chmod / chown as needed
Configuration error	journalctl -xeu mariadb.service	Fix /etc/my.cnf
Disk full	df -h	Free disk space
Port 3306 in use	`ss -tulpn	grep 3306`
SELinux issues	getenforce	Correct context or policy
Interview Questions
1. How do you check whether MariaDB is running?
systemctl status mariadb

or

systemctl is-active mariadb
2. How do you start MariaDB?
systemctl start mariadb
3. Where are the MariaDB logs?
journalctl -xeu mariadb.service
4. Where is the default MariaDB data directory?
/var/lib/mysql
5. Which user should own the MariaDB data directory?
mysql:mysql
6. What command fixes the ownership?
chown -R mysql:mysql /var/lib/mysql
7. How do you enable MariaDB to start automatically after reboot?
systemctl enable mariadb
Real-World Troubleshooting Workflow
SSH into the database server.
Check the MariaDB service status.
Attempt to start the service.
If it fails, inspect the service logs.
Verify ownership and permissions of /var/lib/mysql.
Fix ownership or configuration issues.
Restart MariaDB.
Confirm the service is active.
Verify the application can connect to the database.
Key Takeaways
Always check systemctl status and journalctl before making changes.
Verify ownership (mysql:mysql) and permissions on /var/lib/mysql.
Use systemctl is-active mariadb to quickly confirm the service is running.
